Most samples seem to have "Negative" values of ketone, and the rest of the values are distributed across the different levels of ketone concentration in the urine. Several of them are "Trace" values of ketone concentration in the urine. We document below samples for which ketone concentration is higher or equal than "+1", by visit code and participant.

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(ketone)) |>
  filter(ketone == "1+" | ketone == "2+" | ketone == "3+" | ketone == "4+") |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, ketone) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = ketone, shape = randomized) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(1, 16)) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90)) +
  labs(
    title = "Ketone results",
    subtitle = "Participants with ketone values of 1+ or higher in the urinal data, by visit code and participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant",
    color = "Ketone scale"
  )
```

We document in the table below the randomized participants with ketone values of "1+" or higher.

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(ketone)) |>
  filter(ketone == "1+" | ketone == "2+" | ketone == "3+" | ketone == "4+") |>
  filter(randomized) |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, ketone) |>
  arrange(pid) |>
  gt(
    caption = "Randomized participants with ketone values of 1+ or higher in the urinalysis data"
  )
```

Participant `068100026` has several values of ketone concentration in the urine higher or equal than "1+", across multiple visit codes, and mostly at later visit codes, which is consistent with its high values of glucose concentration in the urine. This participant is randomized, and has type II diabetes as pre-existing conditions. We also notice high concentration of ketone in the urine for participant `068200278`. Participant `068200370` has a single value of "2+" for ketone concentration in the urine at visit code 1500, which happens to be the same visit code at which this participant has a value of "+2" for bilirubin concentration in the urine.

```{r}
