We document below the distribution of **blood** values in the urinalysis data.

```{r}
# summary table

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(blood)) |>
  group_by(blood) |>
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    prop = n / nrow(urinalysis |> filter(!is.na(blood)))
  ) |>
  arrange(desc(n)) |>
  gt(caption = "Distribution of blood values in the urinalysis data") |>
  fmt_number(columns = prop, decimals = 4)
```

We will document below each class of blood concentration in the urine, by visit code and participant, starting with the "Trace" values of blood concentration in the urine.

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(blood)) |>
  filter(blood == "Trace") |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, blood) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, shape = randomized) +
  geom_point(color = "grey20") +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(1, 16)) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90)) +
  labs(
    title = "Blood results",
    subtitle = "Participants with Trace values of blood concentration in the urine in the urinal data, \nby visit code and participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant"
  )
```

Most of the samples with "Trace" values of blood concentration in the urine are from randomized participants, but upon checking, these values may not be due to contamination with menstrual blood, at least according to the reported menstruation status of the participants at the corresponding visit codes.

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(blood)) |>
  filter(blood == "1+") |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, blood) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, shape = randomized) +
  geom_point(color = "dodgerblue") +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(1, 16)) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90)) +
  labs(
    title = "Blood results",
    subtitle = "Participants with values of blood concentration in the urine of 1+ or higher in the urinal data, \nby visit code and participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant",
    color = "Blood scale"
  )
```

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(blood)) |>
  filter(blood == "2+") |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, blood) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, shape = randomized) +
  geom_point(color = "darkgoldenrod1") +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(1, 16)) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90)) +
  labs(
    title = "Blood results",
    subtitle = "Participants with values of blood concentration in the urine of 2+ in the urinal data, \nby visit code and participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant"
  )
```

At each clinical visit, participant `068200379` has systematically a value of +2 for blood concentration in the urine. 
```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(blood)) |>
  filter(blood == "3+" | blood == "4+") |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, blood) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = blood, shape = randomized) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_color_manual(values = c("3+" = "orangered", "4+" = "orangered4")) +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(1, 16)) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90)) +
  labs(
    title = "Blood results",
    subtitle = "Participants with values of blood concentration in the urine of 3+ or higher in the urinal data, \nby visit code and participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant"
  )
```

Several participants, such as `068100029`, `068100036` and `068100049` have overall high values of blood concentration, overlapping with the menstruation status of the participants at the corresponding visit codes (at least for samples comprised between visit code 0000 and visit code 1500).

```{r}
#| fig-height: 6
#| fig-width: 12
# blood

blood_colors <- c(
  "Negative" = "#e3ba3c",
  "Trace" = "#c0be43",
  "1+" = "#91ac45",
  "2+" = "#568745",
  "3+" = "#3d573e",
  "4+" = "#2e4230",
  "Blank" = "grey80"
)

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(blood)) |>
  filter(randomized) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = blood) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_color_manual(values = blood_colors) +
  facet_wrap(~site, scales = "free_x", space = "free_x") +
  theme(
    legend.position = "bottom",
    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90, hjust = 1)
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Blood results",
    subtitle = "Blood values by visit code and randomized participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant",
    color = "Blood scale"
  )

tmp <- urinalysis |>
  group_by(pid) |>
  mutate(
    n_blood = sum(!is.na(blood) & blood != "Negative"),
    n_samples = sum(!is.na(blood)),
    prop_blood = n_blood / n_samples,
    flag_blood = case_when(
      prop_blood == 1 ~ "Consistent blood in urine",
      TRUE ~ "No blood in urine"
    )
  ) |>
  ungroup() |>
  select(
    pid,
    study_day,
    visit_code,
    randomized,
    visit_type,
    site,
    blood,
    n_blood,
    n_samples,
    prop_blood,
    flag_blood
  ) |>
  filter(randomized) |>
  filter(!is.na(blood))

flag_blood <- tmp |>
  filter(flag_blood == "Consistent blood in urine") |>
  pull(pid) |>
  unique()

tmp |>
  filter(!is.na(blood)) |>
  filter(randomized) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = blood) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_vline(
    xintercept = flag_blood,
    linetype = "dashed",
    color = "black",
    alpha = 0.6
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(values = blood_colors) +
  facet_wrap(~site, scales = "free_x", space = "free_x") +
  theme(
    legend.position = "bottom",
    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90, hjust = 1)
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Blood results",
    subtitle = "Blood values by visit code and randomized participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant",
    color = "Blood scale",
    caption = "Vertical dashed lines indicate participants with consistent blood in urine across all attended clinical visits"
  )
```

```{r}

tmp |>
  filter(flag_blood == "Consistent blood in urine") |>
  select(pid, randomized, site) |>
  arrange(pid) |>
  distinct() |>
  gt(
    caption = "Randomized participants with consistent blood in urine across all attended clinical visits"
  )
```

Among randomized participants, 11 participants have consistent blood in urine across all attended clinical visits. Of note, participant `068200229` has reported abnormal uterine bleeding as pre-existing condition, and participant `068100016` has reported uterine infection as pre-existing condition.

We document below the distribution of **nitrite** values in the urinalysis data.

```{r}

# summary table

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(nitrite)) |>
  group_by(nitrite) |>
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    prop = n / nrow(urinalysis |> filter(!is.na(nitrite)))
  ) |>
  arrange(desc(n)) |>
  gt(caption = "Distribution of nitrite values in the urinalysis data") |>
  fmt_number(columns = prop, decimals = 4)
```

Most samples seem to have "Negative" values of nitrite, and some values are "Positive". We document below samples for which nitrite concentration in the urine is "Positive", by visit code and participant.

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(nitrite)) |>
  filter(nitrite == "Positive") |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, nitrite) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = nitrite, shape = randomized) +
  geom_point() +
  # scale_color_manual(values = c("Negative" = "#c9c296", "Positive" = "#844452", "Blank" = "grey80")) +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(1, 16)) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90)) +
  labs(
    title = "Nitrite results",
    subtitle = "Participants with Positive values of nitrite concentration in the urine in the urinal data, \nby visit code and participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant",
    color = "Nitrite scale"
  )
```

Participants `068200225`, `068200347`, `068200407`, `068200439` and `068200441` have been measured with "Positive" values of nitrite concentration in the urine at multiple occurrences, and all of them are randomized. None of them has reported pre-existing condition.

```{r}
