We document below the distribution of **pH** values in the urinalysis data. 

```{r}
# summary table

# urinalysis |>
#   filter(!is.na(ph)) |>
#   group_by(ph) |>
#   summarise(
#     n = n(),
#     prop = n / nrow(urinalysis |> filter(!is.na(ph)))
#   ) |>
#   arrange(desc(n)) |>
#   gt(caption = "Distribution of pH values in the urinalysis data") |>
#   fmt_number(columns = prop, decimals = 4)

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(ph)) |>
  group_by(ph) |>
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    prop = n / nrow(urinalysis |> filter(is.na(ph)))
  ) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(x = ph, y = prop) +
  geom_col() +
  geom_label(
    aes(label = scales::percent(prop, accuracy = 0.01)),
    vjust = -0.5,
    size = 2
  ) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 8, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", alpha = 0.5) +
  geom_vline(
    xintercept = 4.5,
    linetype = "dashed",
    color = "red",
    alpha = 0.5
  ) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom") +
  labs(
    subtitle = "Distribution of pH values in the urinalysis data",
    x = "pH value",
    y = "Proportion of samples (in %)",
    caption = "Vertical dashed lines indicate the normal range of pH values in urine (between 4.5 and 8.0)"
  )
```

According to several references from the American Association for Clinical Chemistry (AACC) or Mayo Clinic Laboratories, the normal range of pH values in urine is between 4.5 and 8.0, with an average value of around 6.0. We document values of pH in the urine that are outside of the normal range, by visit code and participant.

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(ph)) |>
  filter(ph < 4.5 | ph > 8.0) |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, ph) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = ph, shape = randomized) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_shape_manual(values = c(1, 16)) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90)) +
  labs(
    title = "pH results",
    subtitle = "Participants with pH values outside of the normal range in the urinal data, \nby visit code and participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant",
    color = "pH scale"
  )
```

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(ph)) |>
  filter(ph < 4.5 | ph > 8.0) |>
  select(pid, study_day, visit_code, randomized, visit_type, site, ph) |>
  arrange(pid, visit_code) |>
  gt(
    caption = "Participants with pH values outside of the normal range in the urinalysis data"
  ) |>
  tab_style(
    style = cell_fill(color = "#FFCCCC"),
    locations = cells_body(rows = ph < 4.5)
  ) |>
  tab_style(
    style = cell_fill(color = "#ADD8E6"),
    locations = cells_body(rows = ph > 8.0)
  )
```

Most samples with pH values outside of the normal range are from participants from CAP site, and only one sample comes from a non-randomized participant.

```{r}
#| fig-height: 5
#| fig-width: 12
# pH

ph_colors <- c(
  "#E8613A",
  "#E8A83A",
  "#D4C44A",
  "#A8B84A",
  "#7A9E4A",
  "#4A8A6A",
  "#2A7A7A"
)

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(ph)) |>
  filter(randomized) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = ph) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_color_gradientn(
    colors = ph_colors,
