We document below the distribution of the continuous variable **urobilinogen** in the urinalysis data.

```{r}
# summary plot

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(urobilinogen)) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(x = urobilinogen) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 70) +
  geom_label(
    stat = "count",
    aes(label = after_stat(count)),
    vjust = -0.5,
    size = 2
  ) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = 2, linetype = "dashed", color = "red", alpha = 0.5) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom") +
  labs(
    subtitle = "Distribution of urobilinogen values in the urinalysis data",
    x = "Urobilinogen value",
    y = "Count of samples",
    caption = "Vertical dashed line indicates the normal range of urobilinogen values in urine (below 2 mg/dL)"
  )
```
::: {.callout-warning}
Most samples had urobilinogen values above the normal range (above 2 mg/dL). I am not sure whether the units are mg/dL, mg/L or µmol/L. In the first scenario, 33 mg/dL or 66 mg/dL seems unrealistic; it may also be an encoding issue, as we expect values between 0.2 and 8 mg/dL.
:::


```{r}
#| fig-height: 4
#| fig-width: 10

# urobilinogen

urobilinogen_colors <- c(
  "#E8A882",
  "#E89882",
  "#E88080",
  "#D86070",
  "#C84068",
  "#8B1a3a"
)

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(urobilinogen)) |>
  filter(randomized) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = urobilinogen) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_color_gradientn(
    colors = urobilinogen_colors,
    values = scales::rescale(log(c(0.2, 1, 2, 4, 8, 66)))
  ) +
  facet_wrap(~site, scales = "free_x", space = "free_x") +
  theme(
    legend.position = "bottom",
    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90, hjust = 1)
  ) +
  labs(
    title = "Urobilinogen results",
    subtitle = "Urobilinogen values by visit code and randomized participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant",
    color = "Urobilinogen scale"
  )
```

