We document below the distribution of **specific gravity** values in the urinalysis data.

This variable is a measure of the concentration of solutes in the urine, and it is used to assess the hydration status of the participant. The normal range of specific gravity values in urine is between 1.005 and 1.030, with an average value of around 1.015. 

Two variables are available in the urinalysis data: `specific_gravity_value`, which is a continuous variable, and `specific_gravity_signs`, which is a categorical variable with values such as ">=" or "<=".

```{r}
# summary plot

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(specific_gravity_value)) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(x = specific_gravity_value) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 70) +
  # geom_label(stat = "count", aes(label = after_stat(count)), vjust = -0.5, size = 2) +
  # geom_vline(xintercept = c(1.005, 1.025), linetype = "dashed", color = "red", alpha = 0.5) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom") +
  labs(
    subtitle = "Distribution of specific gravity values in the urinalysis data",
    x = "Specific gravity value",
    y = "Count of samples"
  )
```

We see an very low specific gravity value, which may be an encoding issue. We document this value below.

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(specific_gravity_value)) |>
  filter(specific_gravity_value < 0.5) |>
  select(
    pid,
    study_day,
    visit_code,
    randomized,
    visit_type,
    site,
    specific_gravity_value
  ) |>
  arrange(pid, visit_code) |>
  gt(
    caption = "Participants with specific gravity values below the normal range in the urinalysis data"
  )
```


For this non-randomized participant, there is only one measured value of specific gravity, which seems very low, but al of the other constants in the urinalysis data for this participant are within the normal range, and there is no documented preexisting condition.

::: {.callout-warning}
This value of specific gravity seems to be an outlier, and it may be an encoding issue, but I cannot be sure.
:::

We document the distribution of specific gravity values in the urine excluding this potential outlier, by visit code and participant.

```{r}

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(specific_gravity_value)) |>
  filter(specific_gravity_value >= 0.5) |>
  select(
    pid,
    study_day,
    visit_code,
    randomized,
    visit_type,
    site,
    specific_gravity_value
  ) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(x = specific_gravity_value) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 70) +
  geom_label(
    stat = "count",
    aes(label = after_stat(count)),
    vjust = -0.5,
    size = 2
  ) +
  geom_vline(
    xintercept = c(1.005, 1.025),
    linetype = "dashed",
    color = "red",
    alpha = 0.5
  ) +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom") +
  labs(
    subtitle = "Distribution of specific gravity values in the urinalysis data (excluding potential outlier)",
    x = "Specific gravity value",
    y = "Count of samples",
    caption = "Vertical dashed lines indicate the normal range of specific gravity values in urine (between 1.005 and 1.025)"
  )
```

We now document the specific gravity with the `specific_gravity_signs` variable, by visit code and participant.

```{r}
# summary table

urinalysis |>
  filter(!is.na(specific_gravity_signs)) |>
  group_by(specific_gravity_signs) |>
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    prop = n / nrow(urinalysis |> filter(!is.na(specific_gravity_signs)))
  ) |>
  arrange(desc(n)) |>
  gt(
    caption = "Distribution of specific gravity signs in the urinalysis data"
  ) |>
  fmt_number(columns = prop, decimals = 4)
```



