### Pregnancy test results

In this study, two types of pregnancy tests were performed: urine pregnancy test and blood BHCG pregnancy test. We document below the distribution of the results of these two types of pregnancy tests.

```{r}

pregnancy <-
  mae@metadata$visits_crfs_merged |>
  select(
    uid,
    pregnancy_not_applicable,
    pregnancy_test_urine,
    pregnancy_test_bhcg
  ) |>
  left_join(
    mae@colData |>
      as_tibble() |>
      select(uid, pid, study_day, visit_code, visit_type, randomized, site),
    by = join_by(uid)
  )
```

We first document the variable for **urine pregnancy test**, which is a categorical variable with values "Negative", "Positive", "Indeterminate" or "Blank", as documented in CRF3. We document the distribution of these values in the urine pregnancy test data, and then we document the samples with "Positive" or "Indeterminate" values of urine pregnancy test results, by visit code and participant.

```{r}
# summary table for urine pregnancy test

pregnancy |>
  filter(!is.na(pregnancy_test_urine)) |>
  group_by(pregnancy_test_urine) |>
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    prop = n / nrow(pregnancy |> filter(!is.na(pregnancy_test_urine)))
  ) |>
  arrange(desc(n)) |>
  gt(
    caption = "Distribution of urine pregnancy test results in the pregnancy data"
  ) |>
  fmt_number(columns = prop, decimals = 4)
```

Only 3 samples have "Positive" values of urine pregnancy test results. We document these samples below.

```{r}

pregnancy |>
  filter(!is.na(pregnancy_test_urine)) |>
  filter(pregnancy_test_urine == "Positive") |>
  select(
    pid,
    study_day,
    visit_code,
    randomized,
    visit_type,
    site,
    pregnancy_test_urine
  ) |>
  gt(
    caption = "Participants with Positive values of urine pregnancy test results in the pregnancy data"
  )
```

Those three values of positive urine pregnancy test results are from non-randomized participants, measured at baseline visit 0000.

```{r}
#| fig-height: 5
#| fig-width: 12

pregnancy |>
  filter(!is.na(pregnancy_test_urine)) |>
  filter(randomized) |>
  ggplot() +
  aes(y = visit_code, x = pid, color = pregnancy_test_urine) +
  geom_point() +
  scale_color_manual(
    values = c(
      "Negative" = "#c9c296",
      "Positive" = "#844452",
      "Indeterminate" = "#E8A882",
      "Blank" = "grey80"
    )
  ) +
  facet_wrap(~site, scales = "free_x", space = "free_x") +
  theme(legend.position = "bottom", axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90)) +
  labs(
    title = "Urine pregnancy test results",
    subtitle = "Urine pregnancy test values by visit code and randomized participant",
    y = "Visit code",
    x = "Participant",
    color = "Urine pregnancy test results"
  )
```


We now document the variable for **blood BHCG pregnancy test**. We document the distribution of these values in the blood pregnancy test data.

```{r}
# summary table for blood BHCG pregnancy test

pregnancy |>
  filter(!is.na(pregnancy_test_bhcg)) |>
  group_by(pregnancy_test_bhcg) |>
  summarise(
    n = n(),
    prop = n / nrow(pregnancy |> filter(!is.na(pregnancy_test_bhcg)))
  ) |>
  arrange(desc(n)) |>
  gt(
    caption = "Distribution of blood BHCG pregnancy test results in the pregnancy data"
  ) |>
  fmt_number(columns = prop, decimals = 4)
```

Only "Non applicable" and "Blank" levels are available in this variable, which makes sense since BCGH blood tests were only performed if urine pregnancy results were indeterminate.

